# hopf_tori: the torus fields inside a spinor, drawn on the scope.
#
# A direction is a spinor (xi, eta), two complex numbers, and the
# direction it points is its Hopf map (notes/spinors-2026-11-05.md). But
# a direction doesn't fix its spinor: turn both halves together by any
# phase, (xi, eta) e^(i alpha), and it points the same way. So over every
# direction there is a whole circle of spinors, its fibre. A spinor is
# four numbers on a sphere one rung up (the 3-sphere); carried to
# ordinary space by stereographic projection, each fibre is a circle, and
# any two of them are linked, once. The fibres over one latitude of the
# direction sphere lie on a torus, wound round it as Villarceau circles;
# three latitudes make three nested tori, every circle linked with every
# other. Nothing here is drawn as a torus: the tori are what spinors are.
#
# The beam runs round one fibre each 1/110 s (you hear it at 110 Hz),
# steps to the next fibre round the latitude, and goes through the three
# latitudes in turn; the view turns slowly so the tori are seen from
# every side.
#
#   ./aither patches/hopf_tori.aither | tools/scope
#   ./aither patches/hopf_tori.aither | tools/scope | tools/play hw:rockchipes8388c 20     # and hear it

let loop = 110                                             # fibres drawn a second
let per = 24                                               # fibres round each latitude
let lats = [0.28, 0.42, 0.56]                              # the latitudes, as fractions of a half turn from the pole

# Which fibre the beam is on, and how far round it.
let k = floor(t * loop)
let alpha = TAU * ((t * loop) mod 1)
let fibre = k mod (per * 3)
let theta = PI * lats[floor(fibre / per)]                  # the latitude
let phi = TAU * (fibre mod per) / per                      # where round it

# The spinor: one direction's spinors, turned together by alpha.
let xi = rotate([cos(theta / 2), 0], alpha)
let eta = rotate([sin(theta / 2), 0], alpha + phi)

# Stereographic projection of the four numbers into ordinary space, from
# the point where eta is i: the circle through it would reach infinity.
let away = 1 - eta[1]
let px = xi[0] / away
let py = xi[1] / away
let pz = eta[0] / away

# The view: turning slowly about the vertical, tipped well forward so the
# tori are seen from the side, breathing.
let turned = rotate([px, py], TAU * t / 29)
let tip = 1.05 + 0.3 * sin(TAU * t / 41)
let tipped = rotate([turned[1], pz], tip)
[turned[0], tipped[0]] * 0.22
